EXIT(1)                        Funny CLI Manual                        EXIT(1)

NAME
    exit — Leave the CLI, because eventually even the terminal needs
           some alone time.

SYNOPSIS
    exit
    quit

DESCRIPTION
    The 'exit' command terminates the CLI immediately and returns
    control to the operating system.

    It does so by calling:

        sys.exit(0)

    which indicates a successful, completely intentional departure.

    No dramatic farewell is printed. No confirmation is requested.
    You simply leave.

ALIASES
    quit
        An alternate name for 'exit', for users who prefer their
        terminal commands to sound slightly more conversational.

BEHAVIOR
    When executed, the command terminates the current process with
    exit status 0.

    Exit status 0 means:

        "Everything is fine. I meant to do that."

    Any unsaved work or state maintained by the CLI is not handled
    by this command itself. If something needed saving, hopefully
    you remembered before pressing the big red button.

ARGUMENTS
    None.

    Any supplied arguments are ignored by this function.

EXAMPLES
    > exit
        The CLI closes successfully.

    > quit
        Does exactly the same thing.

        Different word. Same escape hatch.

    > exit
        You leave the CLI.

        The terminal remains.
        Your problems remain.
        At least this process doesn't.

FUN FACTS
    - 'exit' terminates the process with status code 0.
    - 'quit' is its alias.
    - No confirmation prompt is displayed.
    - No goodbye message is printed.
    - Exit status 0 tells the operating system that everything
      ended successfully.
    - Sometimes the most powerful command is the one that simply
      knows when to leave.

SEE ALSO
